# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/003_signed_unsigned_comparison.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 003_signed_unsigned_comparison
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Comparison across signedness where the common type changes the result, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every comparison is well defined and every operand is within range. No arithmetic is
performed that could overflow a signed type: the only additions appear inside unevaluated
_Generic controlling expressions, and every printed comparison yields 0 or 1. Conversion
of -1 to unsigned int is modular and therefore defined, not undefined. No shift appears.
No aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, no object modified twice between sequence
points, at most one side-effecting argument per call, no dependence on padding bytes.
Authoring constraint recorded for reviewers: a bare implicit signed/unsigned comparison is
rejected by -Wsign-compare, which -Wextra supplies and which this program's gate keeps, so
each property is expressed through promotion of a narrow unsigned operand, through a wider
signed common type, through a signed operand masked provably non-negative, through an
explicit conversion, or inside an unevaluated _Generic controlling expression. The feature
is fully exercised; nothing is excluded and no warning-gate deviation is taken.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Plain char is never used and no object is declared plain char, because plain char
signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64.
long and unsigned long are never used, because their width is 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes
on the other three targets. No pointer value is printed.
int is 32 bits and long long is 64 bits on all four supported targets, so long long
represents every unsigned int value everywhere and the wider-signed-wins results are
stable across backends.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_lt_ushort=1
fold_lt_uchar=1
fold_lt_llong_uint=1
fold_gt_ullong=1
fold_lt_int_uint=0
fold_eq_int_uint=1
fold_uac_sensitive=1
run_lt_ushort=1
run_lt_uchar=1
run_lt_llong_uint=1
run_gt_ullong=1
run_lt_int_uint=0
run_eq_int_uint=1
run_uac_sensitive=1
common_int_uint=unsigned int
common_llong_uint=long long
common_llong_ullong=unsigned long long
END
